Methods such as toUpperCase return a result rather than rewriting the original String object. Assign a returned result if you want a variable to refer to it.

toUpperCase() takes no arguments and returns uppercased text using the default locale. These letters give this result; locale-sensitive text can differ between locales. The original String is unchanged:
```java
String original = "moon";
String upper = original.toUpperCase();
System.out.println(original + " " + upper);
```
Output: moon MOON.

